er of rate-hike expectations, with July 2026 CPI at +3.4% year-over-year and core PCE near 3.3%, fueled partly by energy-price spikes linked to geopolitical tensions. The federal funds target range sits at 3.50–3.75%, with the effective rate near 3.63% and markets pricing roughly 58% odds of a 25-basis-point hike at the September 15–16 FOMC meeting. Recent labor-market softening, including a July payroll decline of 23,000, tempers the case for action, yet Chair Kevin Warsh’s hawkish tone and several regional presidents’ dissents keep near-term hike probabilities elevated. Traders are watching the upcoming September data releases and FOMC projections for clearer signals on whether sticky inflation will override cooling employment trends.","context_requires_regen":true,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-31T00:46:27.913Z"},"version":"v1"}]
